---
intent: >
  Author the new `brain-aide.md` canonical methodology doc, register it in
  the canonical-doc registry so `installMethodologyDocs` ships it to host
  projects automatically, list it in `index.md`, and update the orchestrator
  command files (`aide.md`, `aide/brain.md`) so the boot sequence and
  brain-config flow speak the new five-state vocabulary and route the user
  to `npx aidemd-mcp sync` for drift recovery.
---

## Project Structure

The `initContent` module's job is unchanged — it remains the single chokepoint
reader/registry for canonical docs. This plan adds one entry to the registry
and adds one canonical doc file under `.aide/docs/`. The orchestrator command
files (`aide.md`, `aide/brain.md`) are also canonical docs registered here;
this plan edits their bodies so they teach the new five-state brain status
vocabulary and the sync workflow.

```
src/service/install/initContent/
├── .aide                                   ← [existing] intent spec
├── plan.aide                               ← [this file]
├── index.ts                                ← [edit] add `brain-aide` entry to DOC_PATHS, append it to METHODOLOGY_DOCS
│                                              public surface unchanged:
│                                                readCanonicalDoc(name: CanonicalDocName): string
│                                                listMethodologyDocs(): readonly MethodologyDocEntry[]
│                                                listAgentDocs(): readonly AgentDocEntry[]
│                                                listSkillDocs(): readonly SkillDocEntry[]
├── index.test.ts                           ← [edit] new test asserting `brain-aide` is enumerated by listMethodologyDocs
│                                              and that readCanonicalDoc("brain-aide") returns non-empty content
└── todo.aide                               ← [existing, untouched]
```

Files outside this module that this plan also edits (canonical-doc bodies that
flow through the registry):

```
.aide/docs/
├── brain-aide.md                           ← [new] canonical methodology doc — schema/lifecycle of the brain.aide file type
└── index.md                                ← [edit] add a bullet linking to brain-aide.md so the host-side hub lists it

.claude/commands/
├── aide.md                                 ← [edit] boot sequence: branch on five-state vocabulary
│                                              (`ok`, `no-mcp-entry`, `no-brain-aide`, `invalid-path`, `mcp-drift`).
│                                              Add `mcp-drift` arm directing user to `npx aidemd-mcp sync` and HALT.
│                                              Add `no-brain-aide` arm directing user to `npx aidemd-mcp init` (cold)
│                                              or `/aide:brain config` (write the file inline) and HALT.
└── aide/
    └── brain.md                            ← [edit] config-mode flow speaks new vocabulary; sync is the
                                              mutation surface for `.mcp.json`; this command no longer
                                              merges `.mcp.json` itself — it scaffolds `brain.aide` and
                                              instructs the user to run sync
```

The orchestrator-command files are canonical docs (already registered under
`commands/aide/aide` and `commands/aide/brain` in `DOC_PATHS`) — editing them
in this plan is the same kind of work as editing `aide-spec.md`. No new code
in `initContent`'s `index.ts` is needed for the orchestrator-command edits;
the registry already enumerates them.

## Prerequisites

None. This plan is the foundation every other refactor plan depends on. The
new `brain-aide.md` defines the file format every consumer reads; the
orchestrator-command edits define the boot vocabulary every consumer's status
must match.

## Plan

### 1. Author `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] Create `.aide/docs/brain-aide.md` as a new canonical methodology doc
      structured parallel to `plan-aide.md` and `todo-aide.md`. The file is
      authored prose — no code in this plan, but the doc body must specify:

      **Heading structure** (mirrors `plan-aide.md` and `todo-aide.md`):
      - `# brain.aide Spec` opener with one-line summary that names brain.aide
        as a sixth AIDE file type and emphasizes the explicit divergence: it
        is a config file, not an intent spec.
      - `## Format` — the YAML frontmatter shape and the body shape, given as
        a fenced example block. Frontmatter fields (typed, required unless
        noted): `connector` (string — descriptive label only, never dispatched
        on by code), `rootPath` (string — absolute path to the knowledge
        store on disk), `entryFile` (string — path relative to `rootPath`
        the agent reads first), `mcpServerConfig` (object: `command: string`,
        `args: string[]` where `args` may contain `${rootPath}` placeholders),
        `tools` (object — semantic-action → MCP tool call mapping; `read`
        and `search` are the floor; users may add more keys for additional
        actions). Body shape: a `## Prose` section containing hand-written
        agent-facing instructions returned verbatim by `aide_brain`.
      - `## Substitution surfaces` — explicit, load-bearing section. Two
        surfaces, partitioned cleanly: (1) `${rootPath}` (and any other
        frontmatter values the user references) inside
        `mcpServerConfig.args`, applied at install time by `npx aidemd-mcp
        sync` only, audience is the `.mcp.json` writer; (2) the `## Prose`
        body, hand-written verbatim per connector, no rendering pass, no
        templating, audience is the agent reading instructions. State the
        invariant: substitution NEVER applies to the prose body.
      - `## Lifecycle` — when the file is created (`aide_init` scaffolds
        the canonical Obsidian default), how the user edits it
        (hand-edits the file directly), how it propagates to `.mcp.json`
        (one explicit user-typed `npx aidemd-mcp sync`), drift
        semantics (boot reporter detects drift between `brain.aide` and
        `.mcp.json`, surfaces `mcp-drift` status, orchestrator HALTS).
      - `## Divergence from intent specs` — name each frontmatter field
        the intent-spec template requires that brain.aide explicitly does
        NOT have: no `scope`, no `description` requirement, no `intent`
        paragraph, no `outcomes` block, no `status` lifecycle field. State
        the rationale: brain.aide is runtime configuration, not an intent
        contract; conflating the two erodes the methodology's central
        distinction.
      - `## Rules` — a bulleted list of invariants. Includes (at minimum):
        prose body returned verbatim by `aide_brain`, no server-side
        templating; `mcpServerConfig.args` interpolation runs only at
        sync time; `tools` floor is `read` + `search`; `connector` is
        descriptive metadata, never dispatched on by package code;
        adding a new brain is a host-side edit + sync, never a code
        change to this package.
      - `## Placement` — fenced example showing `brain.aide` lives at
        `.aide/brain.aide` (project root), parallel to `intent.aide`.

      **Voice and length:** match `plan-aide.md` (~115 lines, decision-form
      prose, examples in fenced blocks). Cite the parent intent spec's
      `outcomes.desired[6]` and Strategy paragraph "Brain configuration
      is user-owned, not code-owned" as the load-bearing constraints.

### 2. Register `brain-aide.md` in the canonical-doc registry

Read: `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] In `src/service/install/initContent/index.ts`:
      - 2a. Add `"brain-aide": ".aide/docs/brain-aide.md"` to the `DOC_PATHS`
        record. Position it adjacent to `"plan-aide"` and `"todo-aide"` so
        the registry reads top-to-bottom in the same order the methodology
        hub introduces the file types.
      - 2b. Append `{ canonical: "brain-aide", hostFilename: "brain-aide.md" }`
        to the `METHODOLOGY_DOCS` array. Position it next to the existing
        `plan-aide` and `todo-aide` entries — `aide-spec`, `aide-template`,
        `progressive-disclosure`, `agent-readable-code`, `automated-qa`,
        `plan-aide`, `todo-aide`, **`brain-aide`**, `cascading-alignment`.
        Order is the host-hub reading order; this entry slots in after
        the other AIDE-file-type docs and before `cascading-alignment`.
      - 2c. No public-surface changes. `listMethodologyDocs()` continues to
        return the array verbatim; downstream `installMethodologyDocs` picks
        the new entry up automatically. `CanonicalDocName` widens by one
        member through the `keyof typeof DOC_PATHS` derivation.

